{%- comment -%}
  Recently viewed products.

  Usage:
    {% render 'recently-viewed' %}
    {% render 'recently-viewed', heading: 'Pick up where you left off' %}

  Renders the heading and an empty grid, both hidden. app.js reads the
  handles this shopper has accumulated in localStorage, fetches a real card
  for each through sections/recently-viewed-card.liquid, and reveals the
  section only once at least one card is in it.

  Hidden-until-filled matters more than it sounds. A returning shopper with
  nothing stored, or one whose browser refuses localStorage, would otherwise
  get a heading over an empty space — the same failure the fabricated
  reviews left behind once their content was taken out.

  ── What it is for ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The product page already carries four recommendation strips: more from the
  brand, you may also like, best sellers, explore collections. All four are
  about the catalogue. None of them is about this shopper. This is the only
  one that is, and on a catalogue of 1,300 fragrances where people compare
  several before deciding, "the four you just looked at" is the shortest
  route back to the one they liked.

  ── Where the current product goes ───────────────────────────────────────

  Excluded. Showing someone the page they are already on, in a strip of
  things they might want to return to, reads as a bug.
{%- endcomment -%}

<section class="related rv-recent" data-recently-viewed
         {% if product %}data-current="{{ product.handle }}"{% endif %}
         hidden>
  <div class="related-inner">
    <div class="related-head fade-up">
      <span class="label">Pick Up Where You Left Off</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">{{ heading | default: 'Recently <em>Viewed</em>' }}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="products-grid" data-recently-viewed-grid></div>
  </div>
</section>
